\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\title{One RLVR round}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{rlvr}
\tag{diagram}

\p{One response-level RLVR round has the following typed order.}

\tikzfig{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  box/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, align=center, font=\small,
    text width=29mm, minimum height=10mm},
  flow/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]}, thick}
]
\node[box] (task) at (0,1.5) {task $x$};
\node[box] (group) at (4.1,1.5) {rollout group $\mathbf y_x$};
\node[box] (evidence) at (8.2,1.5) {evidence and rewards $(e_i,r_i)$};
\node[box] (advantage) at (8.2,-1.1) {group advantages $\widehat A_i$};
\node[box] (objective) at (4.1,-1.1) {clipped objective $J_{\mathrm{clip}}$};
\node[box] (policy) at (0,-1.1) {updated policy $\pi_{\theta'}$};
\draw[flow] (task) -- node[font=\scriptsize, fill=white, inner sep=1pt]
  {$\pi_b$} (group);
\draw[flow] (group) -- node[font=\scriptsize, fill=white, inner sep=1pt]
  {verify} (evidence);
\draw[flow] (evidence) -- (advantage);
\draw[flow] (advantage) -- (objective);
\draw[flow] (objective) -- (policy);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{The arrows denote different operations. Generation acquires responses.
Verification produces evidence. Credit compares rewards. Optimization changes
the policy. Later sections attach costs and provenance to each arrow.}
